TP.register({id:"lp5-g3",lp:5,grade:"3",mode:"curriculum",checks:4,nextGrade:"4th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="Checking the Flags";const FLU=["Base camp was a ring of red tents on the flat white ice. Every morning the team pulled sleds out along a trail marked with small orange flags. The flags mattered. In a storm, blowing snow can hide everything, and a trail without flags is no trail at all.","Nadia had the job of checking the flags. She buckled on her skis and pushed off while the sky was still gray. One flag, two flags, three. At the ninth flag she stopped. The tenth was gone.","She looked for the tracks of yesterday’s sleds, but new snow had filled them in. Nothing ahead looked like a trail. Nothing looked like anything. Nadia stood still and listened to the wind.","She did not turn back. Turning back would mean the next team out would ski into the same blank stretch with no warning. She counted the flags behind her again to be sure of where she was. Nine flags, and the ninth one stood at the top of a low rise, so she could still see it when she looked over her shoulder.","Then she remembered the ridge. The trail always bent left at the tall ridge of ice, and the ridge was too big for any storm to hide. She skied toward it, found the bend, and pushed a spare flag deep into the snow.","On the radio that night she reported the gap in the trail and the new flag. “Good work,” the captain said. “A trail is only as good as the person who checks it.”"].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="What Theo Heard";const STORY=["Theo had been at the polar station for six days and had not seen one animal. His aunt was a scientist there, and she promised him that the ice was full of life. Theo was starting to doubt it. The ice looked empty in every direction.","On the sixth evening his aunt handed him a pair of headphones. A long wire ran from them down a hole in the ice and into the sea. “Just listen,” she said.","At first Theo heard only a soft hiss. Then a sound came through the headphones, low and curling, like someone humming inside a tunnel. It rose, fell, and rose again.","“What is that?” he whispered.","“A whale,” said his aunt. “It is somewhere under us, talking.”","Theo held the headphones against his ears for a long time. The ice above still looked empty. Now he knew better. That night he wrote in his notebook: “Day 6. Saw nothing. Heard everything.”"].join("\n\n");const DOG_TITLE="The Dogs That Pull";const DOG=["A dogsled is a long sled pulled by a team of dogs. Dogs carried people and supplies across the far north long before any engine did. Some polar teams still use them today.","Sled dogs are built for cold. Two coats of fur trap warm air against the skin. A thick tail curls over the nose like a scarf when the dog sleeps in the snow. The pads of the feet are tough, so sharp ice does not cut them.","A team can have six dogs or more. The two dogs at the front are called lead dogs. They listen for the driver’s calls and set the path for the rest. The dogs closest to the sled are the strongest. That is because they take the hardest pull when the sled is heavy.","A good team can travel much farther in a day than a person walking. Dogs never need fuel, and they do not break down in a storm. They do need food, though. A working sled dog eats far more in a day than a dog at home."].join("\n\n");const BERG_A={title:"Where Icebergs Come From",body:"On land near the poles, snow piles up for thousands of years and presses into thick ice. The ice is so heavy that it creeps downhill, a little each year. A slow river of ice like this is called a glacier. When a glacier reaches the sea, pieces crack off the end and float away. A floating piece of glacier ice is an iceberg. Because it came from snow that fell on land, iceberg ice is fresh water, not salty sea water. Some icebergs are as small as a car. The biggest ones are wider than a city."};const BERG_B={title:"Watching for Icebergs",body:"Ships in cold seas keep a careful watch for icebergs. Only about one part in nine of an iceberg sits above the water. So most of it is hidden below. The hidden ice can stick out far to the side. A ship that sails too close can scrape it. Crews use radar to find icebergs in fog and at night. Planes and satellites map the big ones and radio the positions to ships. When an iceberg drifts into a shipping lane, ships are told to go around it."};const WORDS=["iceberg","snowdrift","refreeze","unsafe","mislabel","harmless","stillness","shipment","breakable","station","information","enough"];const MAST="The weather station stands on a mast beside base camp. Three small cups spin on top, and the faster they spin, the stronger the wind. A thermometer hangs in a white box below them, out of the sun, so the reading is the real air temperature and not the warmth of sunlight. A dial inside the box traces a line on a paper drum, one line for every day. Every morning someone skis out to the mast, reads the dial, and copies the numbers into the camp logbook. The logbook is how the team knows whether the weather is changing.";const PHRASES=[["A slow river of ice like this is called a glacier.","Where Icebergs Come From"],["A thick tail curls over the nose like a scarf when the dog sleeps in the snow.","The Dogs That Pull"],["The pads of the feet are tough, so sharp ice does not cut them.","The Dogs That Pull"]];const phraseCards=()=>`<div style="display:grid;gap:12px;margin:10px auto;max-width:720px">${PHRASES.map((p,i)=>`<div style="border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:12px 18px"><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.4rem">${i+1}. ${p[0]}</p><p class="small" style="margin:4px 0 0">from “${p[1]}”</p></div>`).join("")}</div>`;const CORE_LENGTHS=[["1",0],["1½",2],["2",3],["2½",1],["3",2]];function coreDotPlot(){const x0=70,gap=96,axis=142,r=9,step=23;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${x0*2+gap*4} 196" width="${x0*2+gap*4}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A dot plot of ice core lengths in inches. Above 1 inch there are no dots, above one and a half inches there are 2, above 2 inches there are 3, above two and a half inches there is 1, and above 3 inches there are 2.">`;s+=`<text x="${x0+gap*2}" y="20" font-size="17" ${FONT} text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Ice core lengths in inches</text>`;s+=`<line x1="${x0-34}" y1="${axis}" x2="${x0+gap*4+34}" y2="${axis}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>`;CORE_LENGTHS.forEach((c,i)=>{const x=x0+i*gap;s+=`<line x1="${x}" y1="${axis-8}" x2="${x}" y2="${axis+9}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${x}" y="${axis+30}" font-size="18" ${FONT} text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${c[0]}</text>`;for(let k=0;k<c[1];k++)s+=`<circle cx="${x}" cy="${axis-20-k*step}" r="${r}" fill="var(--sea)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`});s+=`<text x="${x0+gap*2}" y="188" font-size="15" ${FONT} text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)">Each dot is one ice core.</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const FONT=`font-family="Andika,sans-serif" font-weight="700"`;const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.4rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const dogHtml=small=>F.passage(DOG,DOG_TITLE,!!small);const bergHtml=()=>`<div class="texts"><article><h3>Text 1: ${BERG_A.title}</h3><p>${BERG_A.body}</p></article><article><h3>Text 2: ${BERG_B.title}</h3><p>${BERG_B.body}</p></article></div>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;function noteBoxes(a,b){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:260px;min-height:100px;padding:8px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.4rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}</div>`}function splitBar(xs,shade){const w=340,h=96;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w+8} ${h+8}" width="${w+8}" role="img" aria-label="Bar cut into ${xs.length-1} parts that are not the same size">`;for(let k=0;k<xs.length-1;k++)s+=`<rect x="${4+xs[k]*w}" y="4" width="${(xs[k+1]-xs[k])*w}" height="${h}" fill="${k<shade?"var(--gold)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="4"/>`;return s+"</svg>"}function icebergSvg(){return`<svg viewBox="0 0 440 300" width="440" role="img" aria-label="An iceberg with a small tip above the water and a much bigger mass hidden under the water"><rect x="0" y="92" width="440" height="208" fill="var(--sea-soft)"/><polygon points="164,22 212,90 118,90" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="118,94 212,94 286,146 252,248 108,262 46,160" fill="var(--surface)" fill-opacity=".8" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="0" y1="92" x2="440" y2="92" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/><text x="330" y="52" font-size="19" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="middle">above the</text><text x="330" y="76" font-size="19" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="middle">water</text><text x="356" y="196" font-size="19" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="middle">under the</text><text x="356" y="220" font-size="19" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="middle">water</text></svg>`}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Supply Crate Words",short:"Crate Words",parts:[{std:["RF.3.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:75,dir:"Read each word out loud. Look for a part you already know at the start or the end of the word.",desc:"reading longer words with prefixes, suffixes, and more than one syllable",key:["Read each word in order: iceberg, snowdrift, refreeze, unsafe, mislabel, harmless, stillness, shipment, breakable, station, information, enough.","Watch for: compound words (iceberg, snowdrift), prefixes re-, un-, mis- (refreeze, unsafe, mislabel), suffixes -less, -ness, -ment, -able (harmless, stillness, shipment, breakable), the -tion ending (station, information), four syllables (information), and an irregular spelling where ough says uff (enough).","Optional word-part check: ask what re- does in refreeze (freeze again) and what -less does in harmless (without harm)."]}]},{id:2,title:"Share the Rations",short:"Rations",parts:[{std:["3.OA.A.2"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud. For number 1, write the equation on your paper. Then hold it up.",desc:"explaining what a division answer means as equal shares or as a number of equal groups",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Rations are the food the team packs for one day. The team stores them in crates and loads the crates onto sleds.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.array(4,6,"box"),"24 ration crates in 4 equal rows"]),qs:["The picture shows 24 ration crates in 4 equal rows. Write the division equation for the picture. What does your answer tell you?","The team has 35 fuel cans. Each sled takes 7 cans. For 35 ÷ 7 = 5, what does the 5 tell you: how many cans on each sled, or how many sleds?","Tell a story about the expedition for 48 ÷ 6. Your story needs an answer. It tells how many are in each share. What is that answer?","Tell a story for 30 ÷ 5 where the answer tells how many equal groups there are. What is the answer in your story?","Two helpers both wrote 18 ÷ 3 = 6. One says the 6 means 6 crates on each sled. The other says the 6 means 6 sleds. Can they both be right? Tell why."],key:["24 ÷ 4 = 6. The 6 is the number of crates in each row, which is the size of one equal share. Partial: the equation right with no account of what the answer tells you is approaching. (3.OA.A.2)","How many sleds. The 35 cans are split into groups of 7, so the 5 counts the groups. Partial: naming the sleds with no reason from the groups of 7 is approaching. (3.OA.A.2)","A story where 48 things are shared equally among 6, such as 48 ration bars shared by 6 team members. The answer is 8 bars for each member. Partial: a story with the right numbers but no equal sharing is approaching. (3.OA.A.2)","A story where 30 things are put into groups of 5, such as 30 flags planted 5 at each camp. The answer is 6 camps. Partial: a story whose answer counts the size of a share instead of the number of groups does not count here. (3.OA.A.2)","Yes. If 18 crates are split evenly onto 3 sleds, each sled gets 6 crates. If 18 crates are loaded 3 to a sled, it takes 6 sleds. The equation fits both, and the story tells you which one the 6 counts. Partial: saying yes with only one of the two readings explained is approaching. (3.OA.A.2)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Ski the Flag Trail",short:"Flag Trail",parts:[{std:["RF.3.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:175,dir:"Read the story out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Winter grade 3 benchmark: about 97 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, winter 50th percentile). The passage length is set from the highest grade 3 figure on that same table, the spring 90th percentile of about 166 words correct per minute, so the minute ends before the text does even for the fastest reader and the score measures the reading rather than the length of the page.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the quiet of “Nothing looked like anything” and for the captain’s voice at the end."]}]},{id:4,title:"Cut the Ice Core",short:"Ice Core",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.1"],dur:165,dir:"Look at the pictures. Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"naming fractions as equal parts of a whole and as a count of unit fraction pieces",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">An ice core is a long round stick of ice. The team drills it out of the ground to study. Bars A, B, and D are drawn like flat ice cores. Circle C is a round snow sample seen from above.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:28px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(6,1,{width:300}),"Bar A")}${group(F.fracBar(4,3,{width:300}),"Bar B")}${group(F.fracCircle(8,5,150),"Circle C")}${group(splitBar([0,.12,.3,.72,1],0),"Bar D")}</div>`,qs:["Bar A is cut into equal parts. What fraction of the bar is shaded?","Bar B: what fraction is shaded? How many pieces of size 1/4 did you count?","Circle C is cut into equal parts. What fraction is shaded? What is one piece of it called?","Bar D is cut into 4 parts. Can you call one of those parts 1/4 of the bar? Tell why or why not.","How many pieces of size 1/6 make one whole bar? How many of them make 4/6?"],key:["1/6. The bar has 6 equal parts and 1 of them is shaded. Partial: 6 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","3/4 is shaded, and that is three pieces of size 1/4. Partial: the fraction right with no count of 1/4 pieces is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","5/8 is shaded. One piece is 1/8 of the circle. Partial: the fraction right with no name for one piece is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","No. The 4 parts are not the same size. A fourth has to be 1 of 4 equal parts of the whole. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the parts being equal is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","6 pieces of size 1/6 make one whole. 4 of them make 4/6. Partial: one of the two counts given is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Dog Team Facts",short:"Dog Team",parts:[{std:["RI.3.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the text. Then answer out loud. Look back at the text to prove each answer.",desc:"questions about an informational text answered with the exact words in the text",html:()=>dogHtml(true),fig2:()=>F.row([F.objects("dog",6,6),"A team of six sled dogs"]),qs:["What is a dogsled? Read the words from the text that tell you.","How does a sled dog’s own body keep it warm? Give two details from the text.","What are the two dogs at the front called. What is their job? Find the sentences that tell you.","Why are the dogs closest to the sled the strongest ones? Read the sentence that explains it.","The text says dogs do not break down in a storm. What do dogs need that an engine does not need? Find the words in the text."],key:["Reads “A dogsled is a long sled pulled by a team of dogs.” (RI.3.1)","Any two from the text: two coats of fur trap warm air against the skin, a thick tail curls over the nose like a scarf, tough foot pads. The tough pads protect the feet rather than warm them, so accept the first two as the warming details. (RI.3.1)","Lead dogs. They listen for the driver’s calls and set the path for the rest. Points to the two sentences about the dogs at the front. (RI.3.1)","Reads “because they take the hardest pull when the sled is heavy.” (RI.3.1)","Food. Points to “They do need food, though” and the last sentence about how much a working sled dog eats. (RI.3.1)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Load the Dogsleds",short:"Sled Loads",parts:[{std:["3.OA.D.8"],dur:180,dir:"Solve each one on your paper. Write an equation with a letter for the number you do not know. Show your work and hold it up.",desc:"two-step word problems solved with an equation that uses a letter for the unknown",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A depot is a pile of supplies the team leaves out on the trail to pick up later. Rations are the food for one day.</p>`,qs:["Each of the 6 dogsleds carries 9 crates. At the depot the team unloads 14 crates. How many crates are still on the sleds?","The team started with 72 days of rations. They used 18 days of rations in the first month and 25 days in the second month. How many days of rations are left?","The team skied 16 kilometers a day for 5 days. Then a storm pushed them back 12 kilometers. How far are they from base camp now?","There are 48 fuel cans. The team splits them evenly onto 6 sleds. Then each sled burns 2 cans on the first day. How many cans are left on each sled?"],key:["40 crates. Equation with a letter, such as 6 × 9 − 14 = c, or 6 × 9 = 54 and 54 − 14 = c. Partial: 54 stops at the sleds and leaves the 14 out, so it is approaching. (3.OA.D.8)","29 days. Equation with a letter, such as 72 − 18 − 25 = r, or 18 + 25 = 43 and 72 − 43 = r. Partial: 54 takes off only the first month and is approaching. (3.OA.D.8)","68 kilometers. Equation with a letter, such as 16 × 5 − 12 = d, or 16 × 5 = 80 and 80 − 12 = d. Partial: 80 stops before the storm and is approaching. (3.OA.D.8)","6 cans on each sled. Equation with a letter, such as 48 ÷ 6 − 2 = f, or 48 ÷ 6 = 8 and 8 − 2 = f. Partial: 8 stops before the first day's burn and is approaching. (3.OA.D.8)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Under the Ice",short:"Story Qs",parts:[{std:["RL.3.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Look back at the story to prove each answer.",desc:"asking and answering story questions using the exact words in the text",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["How many days had Theo been at the station before the evening with the headphones? Which sentence tells you?","What did Theo hear first through the headphones? Read the words from the story.","How did the sound change while Theo listened? Find the sentence that tells you.","Who told Theo that the ice was full of life? Read the words that show it.","Your turn to be the teacher. Ask one question that this story answers. Start with Who, What, or Why."],key:["Six days. Points to “Theo had been at the polar station for six days and had not seen one animal,” or to “On the sixth evening.” (RL.3.1)","Reads “only a soft hiss.” (RL.3.1)","Reads “It rose, fell, and rose again.” (RL.3.1)","His aunt, who was a scientist at the station. Points to “she promised him that the ice was full of life.” (RL.3.1)","Asks a question the story can answer, such as: Who handed Theo the headphones? What was under the ice? Why did Theo doubt his aunt? (RL.3.1, asks questions)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Fuel Drum Fractions",short:"Fuel Drums",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.1"],dur:95,dir:"Answer out loud. You can draw on your paper to show your thinking.",desc:"unit fractions and fractions of a whole in expedition contexts",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A fuel drum is a big barrel of fuel. It is for the camp stove. The side of the drum is marked in equal parts. So the team can see how much is left.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.fracBar(8,3,{width:380}),"A drum marked in 8 equal parts"]),qs:["The team cuts one ice core into 5 equal pieces to study. What fraction of the core is each piece?","Look at the drum in the picture. What fraction of the drum has been used? How many pieces of size 1/8 is that?","Spot the mistake. A helper says that 2/6 means 6 pieces of size 1/2. Say it the right way."],key:["1/5. One piece out of 5 equal pieces of the whole core. Partial: 5 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","3/8 has been used, and that is three pieces of size 1/8. Partial: the fraction right with no count of 1/8 pieces is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","2/6 means 2 pieces of size 1/6. The bottom number tells how many equal parts the whole is cut into, and the top number counts how many of those parts you have. Partial: saying it the right way with nothing said about what each number means is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Radio Check",short:"Radio",parts:[{title:"Fix the Message",std:["L.3.1"],dur:75,dir:"Each radio message has one mistake. Say the whole message out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (verb tense, agreement, plurals, comparing words, pronouns)",qs:["The dogs runned all the way to the depot.","Two sleds is parked beside the tent.","We bringed extra fuel on the second trip.","This is the more coldest day of the whole trip.","The two pilots left her maps in the plane."],key:["The dogs ran all the way to the depot. (L.3.1, irregular past tense verb)","Two sleds are parked beside the tent. (L.3.1, subject-verb agreement)","We brought extra fuel on the second trip. (L.3.1, irregular past tense verb)","This is the coldest day of the whole trip. (L.3.1, superlative adjective)","The two pilots left their maps in the plane. (L.3.1, pronoun that matches its noun)"]},{title:"Build a Field Note",std:["L.3.1"],dur:65,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (joining sentences, future tense, adverbs, abstract nouns)",qs:["Join these two sentences into one. Use because: The team turned back. The ice was too thin.","Change this sentence so it tells about tomorrow. The team flies to the ice shelf. Then it sets up camp.","Which word fits? The sled moved ____ over the fresh snow. Is it smooth or smoothly?","Which word names something you cannot see or touch: sled, courage, or tent?"],key:["The team turned back because the ice was too thin. (L.3.1, conjunction that joins a cause to a result)","The team will fly to the ice shelf and will set up camp. Both verbs need to change, so accept “will fly and set up” too. (L.3.1, simple future tense)","smoothly (L.3.1, adverb that tells how)","courage (L.3.1, abstract noun)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Does It Make Sense?",short:"Make Sense?",parts:[{std:["3.OA.D.8"],dur:100,dir:"Estimate first. Then solve on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"estimating with rounding and checking whether an answer to a two-step problem is reasonable",qs:["The whole route is 418 kilometers. The team has already gone 186 kilometers. Today they went 95 kilometers more. About how many kilometers are left? Round to estimate. Do not find the exact answer yet.","Now find the exact answer. Is it close to your estimate?","Spot the mistake. The weather station took 236 readings the first week and 198 readings the second week. Then 85 of the readings were lost. A helper says 519 readings are left. Does that make sense? Tell why, then give the right answer."],key:["About 100 kilometers, from 400 − 200 − 100. Rounding to the nearest ten gives about 130, from 420 − 190 − 100. Either estimate is fine. Partial: the exact answer alone is approaching, since an estimate was asked for. (3.OA.D.8, estimates by rounding)","137 kilometers. 186 + 95 = 281, then 418 − 281 = 137. Yes, it is close to the estimate. Partial: 281 stops at the distance traveled and is approaching. (3.OA.D.8)","No. Readings were lost, so the answer has to be smaller than the number taken. The helper added the 85 instead of subtracting it. 236 + 198 = 434, then 434 − 85 = 349 readings left. Partial: saying no with no right answer given is approaching. (3.OA.D.8, checks if an answer is reasonable)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Iceberg Watch",short:"Icebergs",parts:[{std:["RI.3.9"],n:4,dur:85,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"comparing and contrasting the main points and key details of two texts on the same topic",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Both texts below are about icebergs. An iceberg is a big piece of ice floating in the sea.</p>${bergHtml()}`,fig2:()=>F.row([icebergSvg(),"Most of an iceberg is hidden under the water"]),qs:["What is the most important point of Text 1? What is the most important point of Text 2?","Name one key detail that both texts tell you.","Which text would help you answer “Is iceberg ice salty?” Which one would help you answer “How do ships find icebergs at night?” Tell how you know.","Text 2 says most of an iceberg is hidden under the water. Does Text 1 tell you that? What does Text 1 tell you about the size of icebergs instead?"],key:["Text 1: icebergs are pieces of glacier ice that crack off where a glacier reaches the sea and then float away. Text 2: ships have to watch for icebergs and use radar, planes, and satellites to stay away from them. (RI.3.9, compares the most important points)","Both say that icebergs float in the sea and move. Text 1 says pieces crack off and float away, and Text 2 says an iceberg can drift into a shipping lane. Also fine: both say icebergs come in very different sizes. (RI.3.9, compares key details)","Text 1 for the salty question, because it says iceberg ice is fresh water, not salty sea water. Text 2 for the night question, because it says crews use radar to find icebergs in fog and at night. (RI.3.9, contrasts what each text covers)","No, Text 1 never says that. Text 1 says some icebergs are as small as a car and the biggest ones are wider than a city. (RI.3.9, contrasts key details)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Field Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.3.8"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:105,dir:"Read the text. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"taking brief notes from a text and sorting them into given categories",html:()=>`${dogHtml(true)}<p class="big">Make two boxes on your paper. Label them like the boxes below. Write two short notes from the text in each box.</p>${noteBoxes("Built for the cold","How the team works")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">A note is just a few words. You do not need a whole sentence.</p>`,key:["Cold box: two facts, such as two coats of fur, traps warm air, tail curls over the nose, tough foot pads. (W.3.8, sorts evidence into the given category)","Team box: two facts, such as six dogs or more, two lead dogs at the front, lead dogs listen for the driver’s calls, strongest dogs go closest to the sled. (W.3.8, sorts evidence into the given category)","Notes are brief: a few words or a short phrase, not a whole sentence copied out of the text. (W.3.8, brief notes)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Expedition Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Dogsled or skis?"},std:["W.3.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:240,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">A dogsled is a sled. A team of dogs pulls it. It can haul a heavy load. It can travel far in a day. But the dogs need food and care. They need it every single day. Skis let one person glide over the snow. Skis never get tired. They never need food. But you have to pull your own small sled. It comes along behind you.</p><p class="big">Which would you rather use on a long trip across the ice, a dogsled or skis? Write your opinion and give reasons. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Say your opinion in the first sentence","Give two reasons for it","Use a linking word: because, since, also, for example","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion (W.3.1)","Gives reasons that support the opinion, such as the heavy load a dog team can pull or the fact that skis need no food (W.3.1)","Uses linking words such as because, since, also, for example, therefore (W.3.1)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.3.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Teach about sled dogs"},std:["W.3.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:240,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to teach someone how sled dogs are built and trained for polar work. Use facts from the text. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Start with a sentence that names your topic","Give facts from the text, and add a detail to at least one of them","Use linking words: also, another, after that","End with a closing sentence"])}${lookBack("the text",dogHtml(true))}`,key:["Introduces the topic: how sled dogs are built and trained for polar work (W.3.2)","Develops the topic with facts from the text, such as two coats of fur, a tail that covers the nose, tough foot pads, lead dogs at the front, strongest dogs nearest the sled, and adds at least one detail (W.3.2)","Uses linking words such as also, another, and, after that (W.3.2)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.3.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"Your sled is stuck in a snowdrift"},std:["W.3.3"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:240,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">A snowdrift is a deep pile of snow the wind has heaped up. You are out on the ice, far from base camp, and your sled sinks into a snowdrift and will not move. Write the story of what happens next and how you get back to camp. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${pills("First","Next","After that","Finally")}${checklist(["Tell who is there and where it happens","Tell what happens in order","Add a detail that helps the reader picture it, and tell what someone says or feels","Give your story an ending"])}`,key:["Sets up the situation and who is in the story (W.3.3)","Events happen in a clear order, with time words such as first, next, after that, finally (W.3.3)","Uses descriptive details and tells thoughts or feelings, or uses dialogue (W.3.3)","Has an ending that wraps up the story (W.3.3)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Say What You Mean",short:"Word Meaning",parts:[{std:["RL.3.4"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the three lines. Then answer out loud.",desc:"telling literal from nonliteral language and explaining what a nonliteral phrase means",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Three lines from texts in this game.</p>${phraseCards()}`,qs:["Line 1 calls a glacier a slow river of ice. Is that literal or nonliteral? What does it mean?","Line 2 says the tail curls over the nose like a scarf. What does that tell you the tail does?","Which of the three lines is literal. Says exactly what it means?"],key:["Nonliteral. A glacier is not really a river of water. It is thick ice that creeps downhill slowly, so it moves the way a river moves. (RL.3.4)","It keeps the dog's nose warm, the way a scarf keeps a person warm. The tail is not really a scarf. (RL.3.4)","Line 3. Tough foot pads that sharp ice does not cut is a plain fact, said exactly as it is. Lines 1 and 2 both compare one thing to another. (RL.3.4)"]}]},{id:15,title:"The Weather Mast",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.3.2"],n:3,dur:60,read:MAST,dir:"Put your pencil down and listen. Your teacher will read once. Then answer out loud.",desc:"finding the main idea and supporting details of a text read aloud",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">Listening time. Your teacher will read a short report about the weather station at base camp.</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center">A mast is a tall pole. A dial is a face with a pointer that shows a reading.</p>`,key:["Ask: What is the main idea of what I just read? (How the camp measures the weather and writes it down every day. Any wording of that idea counts. Naming only one instrument, such as the thermometer, is a detail, not the main idea.) (SL.3.2)","Ask: Give me two details that support that main idea. (Any two: three cups on top spin faster in stronger wind, a thermometer hangs in a white box out of the sun, a dial traces a line on a paper drum, someone skis out every morning and copies the numbers into the logbook.) (SL.3.2)","Ask: Why is the thermometer kept in a white box out of the sun? (So the reading is the real air temperature and not the warmth of sunlight.) (SL.3.2)","Read the report once at a steady pace. If the student asks, you may read it a second time, and note that you did."]}]},{id:16,title:"Tell It to the Camp",short:"Report Out",parts:[{std:["SL.3.4"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:55,dir:"Talk for about one minute. Speak clearly and not too fast.",desc:"recounting an experience with facts and descriptive details at an understandable pace",html:()=>`<p class="big">Tell me about a time you were outside in cold or wet weather. Where were you, who was with you, and what did you do?</p>${checklist(["Say where and when it was","Tell what happened, in order","Add details: what you saw, heard, or felt","Speak clearly, loud enough, and not too fast"])}`,key:["Names the experience and tells where and when it happened (SL.3.4)","Recounts what happened in an order a listener can follow, with facts that fit (SL.3.4)","Adds relevant descriptive details, such as what it looked, sounded, or felt like (SL.3.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace and at a volume you can hear (SL.3.4)"]}]},{id:17,title:"What the Product Counts",short:"Products",parts:[{std:["3.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the crates. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a product as a number of equal groups and describing a context for a product",fig:()=>F.row([F.array(6,4,"box"),"Crates at the depot"]),qs:["The crates are in 6 rows of 4. What does 6 × 4 mean here, and what does the answer count?","Describe a camp situation for 5 × 7. Say what the 5 counts, what the 7 counts? what the answer counts.","There are 7 tents and 3 cots in each tent. Write the multiplication for the total number of cots and say what the answer counts."],key:["6 groups of 4 crates each. The answer, 24, counts the crates in all. Partial: 24 given with no account of what the numbers mean is approaching. (3.OA.A.1)","Any context with 5 equal groups of 7, such as 5 sleds with 7 fuel cans on each sled. The 5 counts the groups, the 7 counts how many are in each group, and the answer, 35, counts the fuel cans in all. 7 groups of 5 is also correct if the student says it that way. Partial: a context with the right numbers but no account of what each one counts is approaching. (3.OA.A.1)","7 × 3 = 21. The answer counts the cots in all, 7 groups of 3 cots. Partial: the equation right with no account of what the answer counts is approaching. (3.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Round the Distances",short:"Rounding",parts:[{std:["3.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you decided.",desc:"rounding whole numbers to the nearest ten and the nearest hundred",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The team writes every distance in the logbook. Then rounds it for the radio report.</p>`,qs:["The team skied 486 kilometers so far. Round 486 to the nearest ten.","Round 486 to the nearest hundred.","One depot is 350 meters from the tents. Round 350 to the nearest hundred. Tell how you decided."],key:["490. 486 sits between 480 and 490, and the 6 ones put it past halfway, so it rounds up. Partial: 480 rounds down when the ones digit is 6 and does not count. (3.NBT.A.1)","500. 486 sits between 400 and 500, and 486 is past 450, the halfway point. Partial: 400 rounds down when 486 sits past 450 and does not count. (3.NBT.A.1)","400. 350 is exactly halfway between 300 and 400, and a number exactly on the halfway mark rounds up. Partial: 300 rounds down at the halfway mark and does not count. (3.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Fractions on the Line",short:"Number Line",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the number line. Answer out loud.",desc:"locating a fraction on a number line, naming an equivalent fraction, and comparing two fractions",fig:()=>F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:.125,labelEvery:1,width:660,marks:[.25,.75],labels:{.25:"P",.75:"Q"}}),qs:["The line from 0 to 1 is cut into equal parts. How many equal parts, and what fraction is one part?","What fraction is point P? Name it a second way.","Which is greater, point P or point Q? How does the line show you?"],key:["8 equal parts, and one part is 1/8. Each part is one eighth of the whole distance from 0 to 1. Partial: the count of parts right with no fraction for one part is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","P is 2/8, which is the same point as 1/4. The two names land on one spot, so 2/8 and 1/4 are equivalent. (3.NF.A.2 for locating it, 3.NF.A.3 for the equivalent name). Partial: 2/8 right with no second name given is approaching.","Q is greater. Q is 6/8, or 3/4, and it sits farther to the right than P, which is 2/8. On a number line the number farther right is the greater one. Partial: naming Q with no reason from the line is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Measure the Cores",short:"Line Plot",parts:[{std:["3.MD.B.4"],n:4,dur:115,dir:"Look at the ruler and the line plot. Answer out loud. For the last one, get a ruler and measure.",mats:["a ruler"],desc:"reading a length to the nearest half inch on a ruler, generating measurements of real objects with a ruler, and reading a line plot of measurement data",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;gap:18px;align-items:center">${group(F.ruler({length:4,px:88,parts:4,item:2.5,itemLabel:"ice core"}),"Ruler marked in inches, halves and fourths")}${coreDotPlot()}</div>`,qs:["Look at the ruler. How long is the ice core?","Look at the line plot. How many cores measured 2 inches?","How many cores were measured in all?","Now you are the measurer. Find three small things at your table. Something like a pencil. Or an eraser. Or a paper clip. Measure each one with your ruler. Say what each thing is. Say how long it is, to the nearest quarter inch."],key:["2 1/2 inches. The end of the core lands on the half mark between 2 and 3. Partial: 2 inches or 3 inches rounds to a whole and is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)","3 cores. There are 3 marks above the 2. Partial: counting the marks above a different number does not count. (3.MD.B.4)","8 cores. 2 at 1 1/2, plus 3 at 2, plus 1 at 2 1/2, plus 2 at 3. Partial: three of the four stacks counted is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)","Three objects of the student's own choosing, each measured and named with a length. Watch the 0 end of the ruler line up with the end of the object, and listen for a length to the nearest quarter inch (a whole, 1/4, 1/2, or 3/4) with the unit inches said. Write the three objects and lengths on the record sheet. Or: any three objects count, since these are the student's own data and there is no fixed answer. Partial: three lengths given but all rounded to whole inches, or the ruler started at the 1 instead of the 0, is approaching. (3.MD.B.4)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Floor Area",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["3.MD.C.7"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the tent floor. Answer out loud. You can draw on your paper.",desc:"relating area to multiplication and to addition of two rectangles",fig:()=>F.row([F.gridRect(4,6,0),"A tent floor in square tiles"]),qs:["What is the area of this floor in square tiles? How does multiplying tell you?","A second tent floor is 5 tiles by 8 tiles. What is its area?","A storage floor is a 4 by 3 part and a 4 by 5 part pushed together side by side. What is the area of the whole storage floor? Show two ways to get it."],key:["24 square tiles. There are 4 rows of 6 tiles, so 4 × 6 = 24. Counting every tile gives 24 as well. Partial: 24 right with no account of how multiplying gives it is approaching. (3.MD.C.7)","40 square tiles (5 × 8 = 40). Partial: 26 from adding the sides gives the perimeter and does not count. (3.MD.C.7)","32 square tiles. One way: add the two areas, 4 × 3 = 12 and 4 × 5 = 20, and 12 + 20 = 32. Other way: the whole floor is 4 by 8, so 4 × 8 = 32. Partial: the right area with only one way shown is approaching. (3.MD.C.7)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Equal Shares",short:"Equal Parts",parts:[{std:["3.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the two shapes. Answer out loud. Draw the last one on your paper.",desc:"partitioning shapes into parts with equal areas and naming one part as a unit fraction of the whole",fig:()=>F.row([F.gridRect(2,3,0,{cell:56}),"Floor A"],[F.fracCircle(4,0,160),"Sample B"]),qs:["Floor A is cut into parts that all have the same area. How many parts. What fraction of the whole floor is one part?","Sample B is cut into parts that all have the same area. What fraction of the whole is one part?","On your paper, draw a rectangle and cut it into 3 parts with equal areas. What fraction of the whole is each part?"],key:["6 parts, and one part is 1/6 of the whole floor. Partial: the count of parts right with no fraction given is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","1/4 of the whole sample. The circle is cut into 4 parts of equal area. Partial: 4 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","A rectangle cut into 3 parts that look about the same size, and each part is 1/3 of the whole. Cutting into 3 strips or into 3 equal blocks both count, as long as the parts have equal areas. Partial: 3 parts drawn that are plainly not equal in area is approaching. (3.G.A.2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Whiskers and Huddles",short:"Senses",parts:[{title:"Sense, Brain, Act",std:["3-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. For the last one, draw your model on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"using a model to show how an animal takes in information, processes it, and responds",html:()=>`<p class="big">A seal rests on the ice beside its breathing hole. Its whiskers are spread out over the water. A fish swims past below, the whiskers twitch, and the seal slides in after it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("seal",96)}${F.icon("nose",80)}${F.icon("fish",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which part of the seal picked up the information about the fish? What was it sensing?","The seal did not dive after every ripple. What had to happen between the whisker twitch and the dive?","Show this as a model: three stages with arrows between them. A label on each stage."],key:["Its whiskers, which sense movement in the water. Accept touch or feeling the water move. (3-LS1-1)","The information went to its brain, and the brain worked out that this movement was a fish worth chasing and decided to dive. (3-LS1-1)","Three parts in order with arrows: whiskers or feels the water move, then brain, then dives. The order is what is rated. A model with nothing in the middle is incomplete. (3-LS1-1)"]},{title:"The Huddle",std:["3-LS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"building an argument from data that forming a group helps animals survive",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team measured the air around penguins standing out on the ice in a blizzard.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Penguins</th><th>Air right beside the bird</th><th>Body weight lost in a day</th></tr>
     <tr><td>one penguin standing alone</td><td>31 C below zero</td><td>400 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a huddle of two hundred</td><td>20 C above zero, inside the huddle</td><td>120 g</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("penguin",88)}${F.icon("wind",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which penguins stood in warmer air?","Make an argument that huddling helps a penguin survive a blizzard. Use a number from the table.","Penguins take turns moving from the cold outside edge of the huddle to the warm middle. How does taking turns help the whole group?"],key:["the ones in the huddle, 20 C above zero against 31 C below (3-LS2-1)","The claim yes, with the numbers attached: a bird in the huddle stood in air 51 degrees warmer and lost only 120 g in a day against 400 g alone, so it has more left to live on. A yes with no number is not yet an argument. (3-LS2-1)","No single bird has to stay out in the worst cold for long, so every bird in the group gets a turn in the warm middle and the whole group lasts longer. (3-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Traits in the Cold",short:"Traits",parts:[{title:"Passed Down and Shaped",std:["3-LS3-1","3-LS3-2"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Look at both tables. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading trait data for parents and offspring, naming the variation, and telling when the surroundings shaped a trait",html:()=>`<p class="big">Table 1: a pair of penguins with a black stripe under the chin, and their chicks across three years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Bird</th><th>Chin stripe</th><th>Flipper length in cm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>parent 1</td><td>yes</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>parent 2</td><td>yes</td><td>21</td></tr>
     <tr><td>chick A</td><td>yes</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>chick B</td><td>yes</td><td>22</td></tr>
     <tr><td>chick C</td><td>yes</td><td>20</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Table 2: two pieces cut from the same clump of moss and set out on the rock ridge.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Moss piece</th><th>Where it was set</th><th>Spread after one summer</th></tr>
     <tr><td>piece 1</td><td>beside a warm vent in the rock</td><td>14 cm across</td></tr>
     <tr><td>piece 2</td><td>out on the open frozen ridge</td><td>3 cm across</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Both parents have a chin stripe and so does every chick. Where did that trait come from?","The flipper lengths are all a little different. What is that difference called. Does it mean one chick is not theirs?","In Table 2 both pieces came off the same clump. What made one spread 14 cm and the other only 3 cm?","So is how far a moss spreads decided only by the clump it came from? Explain."],key:["It was inherited from the parents. A trait both parents have and every offspring has is passed down. (3-LS3-1)","Variation. They are all still their chicks: inherited traits vary from one offspring to the next, so a group of similar organisms is not identical. (3-LS3-1)","How warm the spot was. Same clump, different surroundings, different spread. (3-LS3-2)","No. The environment shaped it too. Listen for the idea that a trait can be influenced by where a living thing grows, not by inheritance alone. (3-LS3-2)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Stone and Survival",short:"Fossils",parts:[{title:"Clues in the Ridge",std:["3-LS4-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table of rock layers. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading fossil data to say which organisms lived in a place and what the place was like",html:()=>`<p class="big">A bare rock ridge rises out of the ice near base camp. The team chipped samples from three layers and listed the fossils. The top layer is the youngest.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Fossils found</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>no fossils, only ground-up rock</td></tr>
     <tr><td>middle</td><td>fern fronds, a piece of tree trunk, beetle wing cases</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>coral, clam shells</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("leaf",80)}${F.icon("shell",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}</div>`,qs:["Name two organisms that once lived where this ice-covered ridge now stands.","The middle layer has ferns, a tree trunk and beetles. What kind of place was this then? Use the fossils as your evidence.","This place is frozen now, and the bottom layer holds coral. What does that tell you about environments over a very long time?"],key:["Any two the table names: a fern, a tree, a beetle, coral, a clam. (3-LS4-1)","A forest, warm enough for trees and ferns and insects to live. Naming the fossils is the evidence. An answer with no fossil named is not yet using the data. (3-LS4-1)","The environment changed completely, from shallow warm sea, to forest, to ice. A place does not stay the same kind of place forever. (3-LS4-1)"]},{title:"A Useful Difference",std:["3-LS4-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how a variation among individuals of one species gives an advantage",html:()=>`<p class="big">Penguins swim after fish. The team measured flippers and counted catches on a hundred dives.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Penguins</th><th>Flipper length</th><th>Top swimming speed</th><th>Fish caught in a hundred dives</th></tr>
     <tr><td>shorter flippers</td><td>18 cm</td><td>6 km an hour</td><td>22</td></tr>
     <tr><td>longer flippers</td><td>22 cm</td><td>9 km an hour</td><td>51</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which penguins caught more fish?","Explain why a longer flipper is an advantage for a penguin here. Use the table.","The longer-flippered penguins also raised more chicks. How can a trait that helps a penguin catch fish end up giving it more chicks?"],key:["the longer-flippered penguins, 51 fish against 22 (3-LS4-2)","Longer flippers give more speed, 9 km an hour against 6, and a faster swimmer catches more of the fish it chases, 51 against 22. The explanation has to lean on the data. (3-LS4-2)","More fish means the parent is strong enough to feed itself and its chicks, so more chicks live to grow up. Accept any version that links surviving to finding mates or raising young. (3-LS4-2)"]}]},{id:26,title:"The Ice Came Late",short:"Habitat",parts:[{title:"Who Survives, and What Would Help",std:["3-LS4-3","3-LS4-4"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Read the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from data who can survive in a changed habitat, then judging the merit of a solution",html:()=>`<p class="big">The sea ice near base camp formed six weeks later than usual and broke up early. The team recorded what they found through the season.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Living thing</th><th>What happened that season</th></tr>
     <tr><td>floating plankton in the open water</td><td>far more than usual, with more open water and light</td></tr>
     <tr><td>seals that rest on ice</td><td>fewer counted, and many hauled out on rocks instead</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ice algae, which grows on the underside of sea ice</td><td>none found, with almost no ice to grow on</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which living thing survived well that season? Give the evidence.","Name the one that could not survive at all and the one that survived less well.","The team suggests building wooden rafts for the seals to rest on. Would that help the seals? Name one drawback.","A second plan is to stop running the camp’s diesel generator and burn less fuel. Which plan has more merit, and why?"],key:["The floating plankton. There was far more of it, because open water let light in. (3-LS4-3)","The ice algae could not survive at all, none were found. The seals survived less well, fewer counted and resting on rocks instead of ice. (3-LS4-3)","Yes for resting, because a raft gives a seal a dry place out of the water. Then any real drawback: it does nothing for the ice algae or for pups born on ice, rafts have to be built and anchored and may blow away, it helps only the seals right at camp. A claim with no drawback is half of this standard. (3-LS4-4)","Either plan can earn credit. What is rated is the reasoning: burning less fuel works on the cause of the warming and would help every living thing here, but it is slow and one camp alone changes little, while the rafts help a few seals straight away and change nothing about the ice. (3-LS4-4)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Weather and Climate",short:"Weather",parts:[{title:"A Season of Weather",std:["3-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud. For the last one, draw your graph on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading and making a graphical display of typical weather for one season",html:()=>`<p class="big">Average temperature at base camp, month by month, through the polar summer.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Month</th><th>Average temperature</th></tr>
     <tr><td>first</td><td>14 C below zero</td></tr><tr><td>second</td><td>6 C below zero</td></tr>
     <tr><td>third</td><td>2 C below zero</td></tr><tr><td>fourth</td><td>9 C below zero</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("thermometer",88)}${F.icon("igloo",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which month of the polar summer is the warmest here?","Does any month in this table rise above freezing? What does that tell you about this season?","Draw a bar graph of the four months. The bars are going down from a zero line. Label both sides."],key:["the third month, 2 C below zero (3-ESS2-1)","No. Every month is below zero, so even in summer the typical day here stays below freezing and ice does not all melt. (3-ESS2-1)","Four bars hanging below a zero line, with lengths that match 14, 6, 2 and 9 below zero, a label on the month side and a label on the temperature side. A graph with no labels is incomplete. (3-ESS2-1)"]},{title:"Two Parts of the World",std:["3-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information to describe the climates of two different regions of the world",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two regions of the world, one at the bottom of the globe and one at its middle.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Warmest month</th><th>Coldest month</th><th>Hours of daylight in midwinter</th></tr>
     <tr><td>this polar region</td><td>2 C below zero</td><td>34 C below zero</td><td>0</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a region on the middle line of the globe</td><td>31 C</td><td>27 C</td><td>12</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("globe",80)}${F.icon("sun",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which region has almost the same temperature all year?","In the polar region there are zero hours of daylight in midwinter. How does that help explain how cold it gets?","Why do different parts of the world have different climates? Give one reason."],key:["the region on the middle line of the globe, 31 C at the warmest and 27 C at the coldest (3-ESS2-2)","With no sunlight at all for weeks, nothing is warming the ground or the air, so the heat keeps leaving and the temperature falls much lower. (3-ESS2-2)","Any sound reason: how near a region is to the middle of the globe, how much sunlight it gets through the year, how high it sits above sea level, how near it is to an ocean. One solid reason is enough here. (3-ESS2-2)"]},{title:"Ready for the Blizzard",std:["3-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"making a claim about the merit of a design that reduces the impact of a weather hazard",html:()=>`<p class="big">A blizzard wind can tear a tent off the ice. It takes minutes. Before the storm season the team cuts snow blocks. They build a curved wall on the windward side. It stands beside every tent. The wall is a little taller than the tent itself.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tent",88)}${F.icon("wind",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which weather hazard is the snow wall meant to reduce?","Make a claim. Does the snow wall have merit? Give your reason.","Name one thing the snow wall does not protect the camp from."],key:["blizzard wind, which can tear a tent loose (3-ESS3-1)","Yes, with a reason attached: the wall takes the force of the wind before it reaches the tent, and a curved wall turns the wind around the sides. A yes with no reason is not yet a claim. (3-ESS3-1)","Any real limit: extreme cold, wind from a different direction, blowing snow piling up and burying the tent, a whiteout that stops anyone moving, the wall itself drifting over. (3-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Sledges on Ice",short:"Forces",parts:[{title:"Balanced or Not",std:["3-PS2-1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"planning an investigation of balanced and unbalanced forces acting on an object",html:()=>`<p class="big">A loaded sledge sits on flat ice. Two team members pull from the front and two hold a rope at the back. Everyone pulls and the sledge stays put.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sled",96)}${F.icon("pack",80)}</div>`,qs:["Are the forces on the sledge balanced or unbalanced? How do you know?","The two at the back let go. What happens, and what changed about the forces?","Plan an investigation to find out. Is a sledge easier to pull on smooth ice? Or on soft snow? What will you change? What will you measure?","Which parts of the setup do you hold steady on every try?"],key:["Balanced. The front pull and the back pull are equal and opposite, and the sledge staying still is the evidence. (3-PS2-1)","The sledge moves forward. Nothing balances the front pull now, so the forces are unbalanced. (3-PS2-1)","A plan with one thing changed and one thing measured: pull the same sledge over smooth ice and then over soft snow (the change) and read a pull scale, or time the pull over a set distance (the measure). (3-PS2-1)","Any controlled variable that fits the plan: the same sledge, the same load, the same distance, the same puller, the same steady walking pace. (3-PS2-1)"]},{title:"Predict the Next Marker",std:["3-PS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a pattern in motion data to predict where an object will be later",html:()=>`<p class="big">Orange markers stand every 100 m along the trail out from camp. A team member on skis is timed as she passes each one.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Marker</th><th>Minutes since camp</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>12</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in the table.","She is heading for marker 8. At what time should she pass it. How do you know?","What could happen on the trail that would make your prediction wrong?"],key:["One marker every 3 minutes, the same time for each one. She is moving at a steady speed. (3-PS2-2)","24 minutes, because eight markers at 3 minutes each is 24. The reason has to be the pattern, not a guess. (3-PS2-2)","A new force acting on her: the trail rises or falls, the snow turns soft or crusted, a headwind pushes against her, she stops to rest, her sledge gets heavier with gear. A pattern only predicts while nothing changes. (3-PS2-2)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Pulling Without Touching",short:"Magnets",parts:[{title:"Ask About the Pull",std:["3-PS2-3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Your two questions have to be questions, not statements.",desc:"asking questions about cause and effect in magnetic interactions between objects not touching",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team’s compass points the same way every morning, until someone sets the iron camp stove down beside it. Then the needle swings toward the stove and stays there. The stove never touches the compass.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("compass",96)}${F.icon("pan",80)}</div>`,qs:["The stove never touches the compass, yet the needle moves. What does that tell you about this force?","Ask two questions about it that you could actually investigate.","Why does this matter for a team navigating by compass out on the ice?"],key:["The force acts across a distance, with no contact between the objects. (3-PS2-3)","Any two real investigable questions: how far away does the stove have to be before the needle goes back, does a smaller iron object do it, does an aluminum pot do anything, does a thicker mitten between them change it, does turning the stove around change the swing. A statement is not a question, so ask once more. (3-PS2-3)","Iron gear carried near the compass can pull the needle off true and send the team the wrong way, so the compass has to be read away from iron. Accept any answer that links the false reading to the danger of going off course. (3-PS2-3)"]},{title:"A Problem for a Magnet",std:["3-PS2-4","3-5-ETS1-1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud. For the third one you may write your two sentences on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem that magnets can solve, with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Steel tent pegs get pulled loose in a blizzard. They are buried under new snow. Digging with a shovel takes an hour. A sharp spade can slice a tent line.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tent",88)}${F.icon("key",80)}${F.icon("snowflake",72)}</div>`,qs:["Define the problem in one sentence. Begin it: The problem is.","Why is a magnet a sensible idea here? Would the same tool find a buried nylon line?","Write two things your tool must do for you to call it a success.","You may use three things only. One ski pole, one magnet, and two cable ties. It has to work with heavy gloves on. What limits does that put on your design?"],key:["A problem statement about finding buried steel tent pegs under new snow without cutting the lines. The problem has to be about finding the pegs, not about the storm. (3-PS2-4)","The pegs are steel, and a magnet pulls steel without touching it, so it can find and lift a peg from under snow. A nylon line is not pulled by a magnet, so no. (3-PS2-4)","Two criteria, written as what the tool must do: find every peg, reach through deep snow, not cut the tent lines, be worked with gloves on, hold the peg while you pull it up. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Names the limits as limits: only three materials, only one magnet, and it must work with heavy gloves. Materials, time and cost are the constraints to listen for, and a gloved hand is a real constraint on the design. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Compare and Test",short:"Design",parts:[{title:"Three Ways to Do It",std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table and the three rules. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing several possible solutions against stated criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three boot designs were worn for a full day out on the ice.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Toe temperature after a day</th><th>Weight of the pair</th><th>Grip on bare ice</th></tr>
     <tr><td>heavy insulated boot</td><td>21 C</td><td>2.6 kg</td><td>poor</td></tr>
     <tr><td>light trail boot</td><td>9 C</td><td>1.1 kg</td><td>good</td></tr>
     <tr><td>insulated boot with a studded sole</td><td>19 C</td><td>1.8 kg</td><td>good</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The rules for success: keep toes above 15 C, weigh under 2 kg a pair, grip bare ice.</p>`,qs:["Which design keeps toes warmest?","Which design clears all three rules? Explain with the numbers.","Pick one you would not use. Then name the rule it misses."],key:["the heavy insulated boot, 21 C (3-5-ETS1-2)","The insulated boot with a studded sole. Toes at 19 C, 1.8 kg, and good grip. Another choice counts only if the student argues it against all three rules using the table. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any reasoned rejection with the rule named: the heavy boot fails the weight rule at 2.6 kg and the grip rule, the light trail boot fails the warmth rule at 9 C. Naming the rule is the part being rated. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{title:"Make the Test Fair",std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"judging whether a test is fair, naming controlled variables, and identifying a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two tent peg shapes were tested to see which one holds better in snow.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Peg</th><th>Snow</th><th>Pull used to test it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>flat blade peg</td><td>hard packed snow</td><td>a steady pull by one person</td></tr>
     <tr><td>round pin peg</td><td>loose fresh snow</td><td>a sudden hard yank</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Does this test prove anything? Say why.","Name two things to keep the same so the test is fair.","The flat blade peg bent on every single pull. It bent at the point where the line is tied. Name that weak spot. Then say what you would change about the peg."],key:["No. Two things were different at once, the snow and the kind of pull, so holding better cannot be pinned on the shape. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two controlled variables: the same snow, the same kind and size of pull, the same depth driven in, the same angle, the same temperature. (3-5-ETS1-3)","The failure point is the bend at the tie point. Any improvement aimed there: make that part thicker, use a stronger metal, add a reinforcing collar, move the tie hole lower on the blade. An improvement somewhere else does not count. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Maps and Places",short:"Maps",parts:[{std:["HSS-3.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Use the map grid and the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a map grid and a table to describe physical and human features and where they sit",html:()=>`<p class="big">A map grid of the ground around base camp. North is the top row.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>west</th><th>middle</th><th>east</th></tr>
     <tr><th>north</th><td>${F.icon("mountain",54)}<br>ice ridge</td><td>${F.icon("telescope",54)}<br>weather post</td><td>${F.icon("snowflake",54)}<br>snowfield</td></tr>
     <tr><th>middle</th><td>${F.icon("wave",54)}<br>open water</td><td>${F.icon("tent",54)}<br>base camp</td><td>${F.icon("radio",54)}<br>radio mast</td></tr>
     <tr><th>south</th><td>${F.icon("penguin",54)}<br>penguin colony</td><td>${F.icon("sled",54)}<br>sledge trail</td><td>${F.icon("box",54)}<br>supply depot</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>Distance from base camp</th></tr>
     <tr><td>radio mast</td><td>0.2 km</td></tr><tr><td>weather post</td><td>1.5 km</td></tr>
     <tr><td>supply depot</td><td>4 km</td></tr><tr><td>ice ridge</td><td>9 km</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What lies directly east of base camp?","Which two directions do you travel to get from base camp to the penguin colony?","Find one natural thing and one built thing on this map.","Which place in the table is farthest from camp? Name one reason a team would want this table as well as the map."],key:["the radio mast (HSS-3.1)","south and west, in either order. The penguin colony sits in the south row and the west column. Partial: one of the two directions is approaching. (HSS-3.1)","Physical: the ice ridge, the open water, the snowfield. Human-made: base camp, the weather post, the radio mast, the supply depot, the sledge trail. One of each is what is being checked. Partial: one of the two kinds named is approaching. (HSS-3.1)","the ice ridge, 9 km. The map shows which way to go and the table shows how far, so together they let the team work out how long a trip will take and what to carry. Partial: the farthest place named with no reason for wanting the table too is approaching. (HSS-3.1)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Long Ago in This Place",short:"Long Ago",parts:[{title:"The First Nations Here",std:["HSS-3.2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Use what is on the screen and what you have learned.",desc:"describing how the American Indian nations of California used the rivers and traded long ago, and how a nation governs itself today",html:()=>`<p class="big">Some California nations lived along rivers. They caught salmon and other fish. They dried them on racks in the sun and wind. They traded dried fish with other nations. Those nations lived away from the water.</p>
      <p class="big">A nation governs itself. Many California nations have written a tribal constitution. It sets out how their own council and courts work. They deal with the state of California as a government. They deal with the United States government the same way. They are not a club.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("fish",88)}${F.icon("drop",80)}</div>`,qs:["The screen says river nations dried their fish. How did they dry it. Who did they trade the dried fish to?","People here were drying fish and trading it. What does that tell you about how they lived? What does it tell you about working with other nations?","Many nations have written a tribal constitution. What does a nation use one for. Who does a nation deal with as one government to another?"],key:["They dried the fish on racks in the sun and the wind. They traded the dried fish to nations living away from the water. Partial: one of the two halves answered is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","They used what their own place gave them, and they traded with nations who had other things, so nations were connected to each other and not living apart. Listen for the local land and the trade link. Partial: the local food named with nothing said about the trade link, or the reverse, is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","A tribal constitution sets out how the nation governs itself: how its council is chosen and how its own courts and laws work. A nation deals with the state of California and with the United States government, government to government. Accept state and federal, or the state and the country. Partial: one of the two halves answered is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","Not scored, ask it anyway: which American Indian nation or nations have their homeland where this student lives? The answer is different for every family, so there is no key for it and it stays out of the rating. Tell the student the name if they do not know it, write it on the record sheet, and make it something to teach before the next check."]},{title:"A Town Over Time",std:["HSS-3.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the timeline. Answer out loud.",desc:"sequencing local historical events and describing the mark each period left on the land",html:()=>`<p class="big">A timeline for the town of Frost Harbor.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1862</td><td>a wharf is built in the sheltered part of the bay</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1891</td><td>an ice house opens, and a pond is dug to freeze and cut ice in winter</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1924</td><td>electric freezers arrive and the ice house closes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1958</td><td>the bay is dredged deeper so larger ships can dock</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2001</td><td>the old ice pond becomes a bird sanctuary</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Put these three in order, earliest first. The ice house. The dredging of the bay. The wharf.","Which event changed the shape of the bay bottom. What did it do?","Pick one period on this timeline. What mark has it left? Think of the land or the water. Tell me one someone could still see today."],key:["wharf, ice house, dredging of the bay. Partial: two of the three in the right order is approaching. (HSS-3.3)","the dredging in 1958, which cut the bay bottom deeper so larger ships could come in. Partial: naming the dredging with no account of what it did to the bay is approaching. (HSS-3.3)","Any period with a physical mark named: the wharf or its pilings, the dug pond now a sanctuary, the deeper shipping channel, the ice house building if it still stands. Partial: naming a period with no mark on the land or the water named is approaching. (HSS-3.3)"]}]},{id:33,title:"Rules and Work",short:"Rules",parts:[{title:"Rules, Laws and Government",std:["HSS-3.4"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"telling rules from laws and naming the basic parts and levels of United States government",html:()=>`<p class="big">The expedition has a rule: nobody leaves camp alone. Back home there is a law about boats. A boat must carry one life jacket for every person aboard.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("flag",88)}${F.icon("books",88)}</div>`,qs:["What is the difference between the expedition’s rule and the life jacket law?","Why do we have laws at all? Give one reason.","The United States government has three parts. Which part writes the laws. Which part puts them into effect?","Name one level of government bigger than your own town."],key:["The expedition rule is set by the team leader and applies to the team. The law is made by a government, applies to everyone with a boat, and the government decides what happens when it is broken. Partial: describing the team rule with nothing said about who makes the law is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","Any sound reason: to keep people safe, to settle disagreements fairly, so everyone knows what to expect, to protect people’s rights and property. Partial: saying laws are the rules with no reason for having them is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","the legislative branch, Congress, makes the laws, and the executive branch, led by the President, carries them out. Partial: one of the two parts named is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","the county, the state government, or the national government (HSS-3.4)"]},{title:"Choices and the Local Economy",std:["HSS-3.5"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"basic economic reasoning about scarcity, trade-offs, goods and services",html:()=>`<p class="big">Only one supply flight a month reaches the camp, and it can carry 300 kg. The team wants all of this.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What they want</th><th>Weight</th></tr>
     <tr><td>fuel for the stove</td><td>150 kg</td></tr><tr><td>fresh food</td><td>120 kg</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a spare radio</td><td>60 kg</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Can the flight carry all three? Work it out.","They load the fuel and the fresh food. What have they given up. What is the name for the thing you give up when you choose?","Fuel is one kind of thing and the pilot’s flying is another. Which is a good and which is a service?","Name one job where you live. Then say which of the two kinds of work it is."],key:["No. 150 plus 120 plus 60 is 330 kg, and the flight carries 300 kg. Partial: saying no with no total worked out is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","They gave up the spare radio. The idea is a trade-off, or opportunity cost: picking one thing means doing without another, because space and weight are limited. The idea matters more than the term at this grade. Partial: naming what was given up with no idea of a trade-off, or the term with nothing given up named, is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","Fuel is a good, something you can hold and use up. Flying the plane is a service, work someone does for you. Partial: one of the two sorted right is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","Any real local job sorted correctly: a baker makes a good, a mechanic provides a service, a farmer produces a good, a pilot provides a service. Partial: a real job named with the good or service label wrong is approaching. (HSS-3.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;STOPS[2].parts[0].n=WC;STOPS[2].parts[0].key[0]=STOPS[2].parts[0].key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);STOPS[2].parts[0].desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level story read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Dog Team Detective",std:["RI.4.1"],needs:["RI.3.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details and examples from the text.",html:()=>`<p class="big">The text never says what would go wrong if a lead dog stopped listening to the driver. Use details from the text to explain what could happen to the whole team.</p>${dogHtml(true)}`,key:["The whole team would go the wrong way or off the trail. Details and examples: the two dogs at the front are the lead dogs, they listen for the driver’s calls, and they set the path for the rest of the team, so the other dogs follow wherever the leaders go. The text never states this, so the student has to put the details together. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details and examples)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Read Between the Lines",std:["RL.4.1"],needs:["RL.3.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<p class="big">The story never says that Theo changed his mind about the ice. How do you know that he did? Give two details or examples from the story.</p>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["He changed his mind, backed by two details: he held the headphones against his ears for a long time, the story says “Now he knew better,” and he wrote “Saw nothing. Heard everything” in his notebook. At the start he doubted his aunt and thought the ice looked empty. (RL.4.1, inference with details)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Grown-Up Grammar",std:["L.4.1"],needs:["L.3.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which sentence is right? A: The team could of waited one more day. B: The team could have waited one more day.</li><li>Put these words in the order that sounds right in English: <b>three / orange / small / flags</b></li><li>Change this sentence so it tells what is happening right now: The pilot checks the fuel.</li></ol>`,key:["B. “Could of” is not a correct form, so the modal auxiliary needs have. (L.4.1, modal auxiliaries and common usage errors)","three small orange flags. Number, then size, then color, then the noun. (L.4.1, ordering adjectives within a sentence)","The pilot is checking the fuel. (L.4.1, present progressive verb tense)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Three-Step Trek",std:["4.OA.A.3"],needs:["3.OA.D.8"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Then tell your teacher about your estimate.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The team must move 196 crates to the depot. Each dogsled carries 8 crates and makes 3 trips. How many dogsleds are needed to move all 196 crates? Write an equation with a letter for the unknown.</li><li>Before you solve, estimate the answer by rounding, and say whether your exact answer is reasonable.</li></ol>`,key:["9 dogsleds. Each sled moves 8 × 3 = 24 crates. 24 × 8 = 192, so 8 sleds leave 4 crates behind and a 9th sled is needed. Equation with a letter, such as 24 × s + 4 = 196, or 196 ÷ 24 = s with 4 left over. The leftover 4 is what makes the answer 9 and not 8. (4.OA.A.3, multistep problem with a remainder to interpret)","Estimate such as 200 ÷ 25 = 8, or about 24 crates a sled so about 8 sleds, which makes 9 with the leftovers reasonable. (4.OA.A.3, assesses reasonableness using rounding and estimation)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Put It Together",std:["RI.4.9"],needs:["RI.3.9"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud in your own words. Use one fact from each text.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Use both texts to explain why an iceberg that looks small from a ship can still be dangerous. Use one fact from Text 1 and one fact from Text 2.</p>${bergHtml()}`,key:["Pulls the two texts together: from Text 2, only about one part in nine of an iceberg sits above the water and the hidden ice can stick out far to the side, so the part a crew sees is only a small piece of it. From Text 1, icebergs are thick glacier ice built from thousands of years of snow, and even a berg that shows a car-sized tip is a big block of hard ice under the surface. One fact from each text is needed. (RI.4.9, integrates information from two texts to speak about the topic knowledgeably)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.3.1","RI.3.1","RI.3.9","W.3.8","RL.4.1","RI.4.1","RI.4.9"];const MULT_N=10;const PV_COLS=["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones"];const FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8];let multSel=[0,0],pvVal="",fracOn={};function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function pvTable(){const ip=pvVal.replace(/[^0-9]/g,"").slice(-PV_COLS.length);let h="<tr>";PV_COLS.forEach(c=>h+=`<th>${c}</th>`);h+="</tr><tr>";PV_COLS.forEach((c,i)=>{const k=ip.length-(PV_COLS.length-i);h+=`<td>${k>=0?ip[k]:""}</td>`});return h+"</tr>"}function pvHtml(){return`<label class="pv-in">Type a number<input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="pvInput" value="${pvVal}" autocomplete="off"></label><div class="tool-scroll"><table class="pv" id="pvTable">${pvTable()}</table></div>`}function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1 whole":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}function starters(st){if(st.std.includes("W.3.3"))return["Far from camp, ____.","First, ____.","Next, ____.","“____,” I said.","Finally, ____."];if(st.std.includes("W.3.2"))return["Sled dogs are built for ____.","One fact is ____.","Also, ____.","Another fact is ____.","Now you know ____."];if(st.std.includes("W.3.8"))return["The text says ____.","Fur: ____.","Lead dogs: ____."];return["I would rather use ____ because ____.","One reason is ____.","For example, ____.","That is why ____."]}function sentHtml(st){return`<ul class="starters">${starters(st).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["3.OA.A.1","3.OA.A.2","3.OA.D.8","3.MD.C.7","4.OA.A.3"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart",std:["3.NBT.A.1","3.OA.D.8","4.OA.A.3"],html:pvHtml,bind:b=>{const inp=b.querySelector("#pvInput"),tb=b.querySelector("#pvTable");if(inp&&tb)inp.oninput=()=>{pvVal=inp.value.replace(/[^0-9,]/g,"");tb.innerHTML=pvTable()}}},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3","3.G.A.2"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},sent:{name:"Sentence starters",std:["W.3.1","W.3.2","W.3.3","W.3.8"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["3.OA.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the related facts, so rate on whether the student can say what the quotient counts, not on the fact itself."},{t:"mult",std:["3.OA.D.8","4.OA.A.3"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the facts. Rate on the equation, the two steps, and the estimate."},{t:"pv",std:["3.OA.D.8","4.OA.A.3"],msg:"The place value chart lines the digits up for the student, so count the rounding and estimating items as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["3.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the equal parts and the shading, so rate on whether the student can name the unit fraction and explain what the strips show."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.1","W.3.2","W.3.3"],msg:"Sentence starters supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.8"],msg:"Sentence starters give the student the note categories, so rate on the facts chosen and on how brief the notes are."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.3.1","RI.3.1","RI.3.9","W.3.8","RL.4.1","RI.4.1","RI.4.9"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"mult",std:["3.OA.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the product. Rate on whether the student can say what the groups are and what the answer counts, not on the fact."},{t:"mult",std:["3.MD.C.7"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the product, so rate on whether the student connects rows times columns to the number of square tiles."},{t:"pv",std:["3.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The place value chart lines the digits up for the student, so count the rounding items as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3","3.G.A.2"],msg:"Fraction strips show the equal parts side by side, which is most of what these items ask the student to work out. Note that the answer was found with a model."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.3.4"],msg:"If the three lines were read to the student, this stop shows listening, not reading. The meaning answers still count."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
